// ============================================================================
// WT-01 — witness 2: a JSON parser (RFC 8259).
//
// THIS FILE DOES NOT SATISFY WT-01. WT-01 asks for a JSON parser written with
// NO WORKAROUNDS. This one runs, and it is built out of four of them. Every one
// is marked `// WORKAROUND <row>:` at the point of use — four such comments
// below this header, four distinct workarounds, one per comment. Three more
// comments are marked `// WORKAROUND DISCHARGED` and are not among them: they
// record the constant workaround that N3-09/N3-10 removed and the two cursor
// workarounds the method rewrite removed, so that the count's history stays
// readable at the site it changed. Nine more sites carry a
// shorter `// <row> DISCHARGED` note for the same reason. `<row>` is the
// requirement id that owns the missing construct, or the word UNOWNED, which
// means the construct that would remove the workaround is declared by NOTHING in
// docs/contributing/1.0-requirements.tsv. THREE of the four are UNOWNED, over
// three distinct gaps, and those are the ones a roadmap reading of N5/N6 cannot
// see. Exactly one of the four — the arena — is owned outright.
//
// The count has moved eight times, and every move is a fact about the compiler
// rather than a rewording here. `cfa7e7f` gave recursive types a layout and a
// diagnostic, closing one UNOWNED workaround. Two more closed when the claims
// under them were re-measured against the compiler that is actually here:
// char literals lex (N2-04, `satisfied`), so twenty-nine of the thirty-one
// zero-arg byte-code functions are gone — `ch_backspace` and `ch_formfeed`
// stay because `'\b'` and `'\f'` are a compile error, and that is the closed
// escape set behaving correctly, not a workaround; and `"\\t"` lexes to
// backslash + `t` (N2-09, `satisfied`), so the hand-assembled backslash is
// gone. One move went the other way. The number workaround was tagged N4-02,
// which is `satisfied` — `f64` exists — so the tag was wrong. What is actually
// missing is any way to move a float ACROSS the text boundary, in EITHER
// direction: no `string_to_float` and no `parse_float`, so a lexeme cannot
// become a value, and no `float_to_string` and no `print_float`, so a value
// cannot be printed. No row owns either direction, so re-tagging it UNOWNED
// raised that count from three to four.
//
// The fifth and sixth moves are the ones where the missing construct ARRIVED
// rather than a claim being re-measured. The fifth: M2 item 9 landed top-level
// `const` and `static`, the two zero-arg constant functions became `const`
// items, and the count fell fourteen -> thirteen. The sixth was the round
// before this one, and it is the largest single move the file has taken: N5-06, N5-07, N5-12 and
// N5-13 are all `satisfied`, and all four were measured to be so HERE, in this
// program, not in a fixture — so the workarounds were APPLIED AWAY rather than
// re-labelled. Chained `else` replaced seven `else { if … }` staircases
// carrying nineteen arms between them, the deepest nine branches and eight
// levels deep; `loop` replaced four `while true`; `<<`, `|`, `>>` and `&`
// replaced the multiply/divide/modulo arithmetic at both the hex-digit and the
// UTF-8 site, which is what removed the duplicate marker; and `+=`/`-=`
// replaced ten read-modify-write statements.
// The count fell thirteen -> nine and the transcript
// tests/witness/json_parser.expected did not change by one byte, which is the
// point: a discharge that moved the output would be a rewrite, not a discharge.
//
// That round also re-measured four more markers and found three of their tags
// wrong in the SAME direction — the row named was `satisfied`, and the thing
// actually missing was owned by nobody. `match` as an expression (N5-04),
// literal patterns (N6-02), range patterns (N6-03) and or-patterns (N6-07) all
// work over `i64` scrutinees, measured. This parser matches on `char`, and a
// char literal was not a pattern: `'n' => …` was "Expected pattern, but found
// char 'n'". One gap, three sites, no owner — the fifth UNOWNED finding.
//
// THE SEVENTH MOVE CLOSED THAT GAP, and it is the second time a construct
// ARRIVED for this file rather than a claim being re-measured. A char literal is
// a pattern now, and a char range orders by code point, so all three sites were
// APPLIED: `js_is_ws` is the or-pattern `' ' | '\t' | '\n' | '\r'`, `js_is_digit`
// is the range `'0'..='9'`, and the dispatch in `value` (then `js_value`) —
// the deepest staircase in the file, nine branches with a mutable local and one
// assignment per arm — is a `match` EXPRESSION whose value is the node. The
// count fell nine -> six, the UNOWNED count seven -> four, and the transcript
// did not change by one byte. The construct is still owned by NOTHING in the requirements manifest,
// which is parked as issue #46: the gap closed, its ownership question did not.
// The fourth re-measurement went the
// other way and closed nothing: method call syntax (N5-17) works now, so the
// cursor marker was re-tagged to what actually blocks it, a receiver that can
// be written through and called more than once.
//
// THE EIGHTH MOVE IS THE CURSOR, and it is the third time a construct ARRIVED
// for this file. su2 (`b54502b`) gave `&self` and `&mut self` receivers a real
// pointer and, through the same predicate, made a `&T` parameter forwardable;
// what was left was the rewrite, and the rewrite is done. Every reader in the
// parser is a `&self` method, every writer a `&mut self` method, and every
// call into it is `j.thing(...)` or `self.thing(...)`. Both cursor markers are
// discharged at the Cursor section below. Applying them found two borrow-checker
// defects, fixed in their own units (W1c, W1d) on the `main` this compiles on.
// The count fell six -> four, the UNOWNED count four -> three, and the
// transcript did not change by one byte. One cost is measured, not hidden:
// effect inference walks free functions only and adds nothing for a method
// call, so the parser's methods are no longer in the compiler's effects report.
// That is N7-07 (M5, `owed`), the compiler's gap rather than a workaround here.
//
// The shape this file would have if nothing were missing is written out in
// tests/witness/json_parser.no-workarounds.md, beside a per-workaround diff, so
// that when the remaining rows land the claim "the workarounds are gone" can be
// CHECKED rather than re-derived. Items 3, 4 and 9 have landed, and so have the
// char patterns and the method receivers; WT-01 stays `owed` because four
// workarounds remain, three of them UNOWNED.
//
// What it does: parses one JSON document into a flat arena, re-serialises it
// canonically, answers structural queries against it, and refuses fifteen
// malformed documents at a named byte offset. Fifteen is checkable rather than
// claimed: `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]*show_err\(j,' tests/witness/json_parser.pd`
// is 15 — the call sites in `main`, anchored so that this sentence does not
// count itself — and the pinned transcript carries fifteen lines after its
// `-- refused --` marker. The transcript is tests/witness/json_parser.expected;
// every line of it is checkable by reading the input strings in `main` below.
// ============================================================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Named constants.
//
// WORKAROUND DISCHARGED (N3-09, N3-10): these were `fn cap_nodes() -> i64` and
// `fn max_depth() -> i64`, zero-arg functions standing in for constants because
// `const LIMIT: i64 = 10;` was "Expected function, struct, enum, trait, type,
// impl, or macro declaration". Top-level `const` items exist now, so each use is
// a name again rather than a call. This is the first workaround in this file
// removed by the construct ARRIVING rather than by re-measuring a claim.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const CAP_NODES: i64 = 192;
const MAX_DEPTH: i64 = 24;

// Node kinds.
//
// WORKAROUND UNOWNED: these are an `enum` in any language that has one, and
// Palladium HAS enums — but an array of them cannot be built. Both
// `[K::A; 4]` and `[K::A, K::A, K::A, K::A]` are refused with
// "Type mismatch: expected [K; 4], found [K; 4]", a diagnostic that names the
// same type on both sides. N4-09 is `satisfied` and states it is witnessed for
// `[i64; 5]`, `[i64; 3]`, `[String; 3]` only, so it does not cover this; no
// other row does either. The arena therefore stores kinds as i64.
fn k_null() -> i64 { return 0; }
fn k_false() -> i64 { return 1; }
fn k_true() -> i64 { return 2; }
fn k_number() -> i64 { return 3; }
fn k_string() -> i64 { return 4; }
fn k_array() -> i64 { return 5; }
fn k_object() -> i64 { return 6; }

// The two character codes with no literal form.
//
// The escape set is closed: `\n \t \r \" \\ \'` in a string literal, those
// plus `\0` in a char literal (N2-09, satisfied). `'\b'` and `'\f'` are an
// "unknown escape sequence" compile error. JSON names both escapes, so these
// two bytes -- and only these two -- are written here as decimals. Every other
// character this parser dispatches on is written as the char literal it is.
fn ch_backspace() -> char { return 8 as char; }
fn ch_formfeed() -> char { return 12 as char; }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The document.
//
// WORKAROUND N14-09 + N4-12 + N4-15: the natural representation is
//
//     enum Json { Null, Bool(bool), Number(f64), Str(String),
//                 Array(Vec<Json>), Object(Vec<(String, Json)>) }
//
// and it is still not reachable — but as of cfa7e7f the recursion is no longer
// what stops it.
//   * The recursive variants WORK. `enum V { Leaf(i64), Pair(V, V) }` compiles,
//     links and runs: a self-recursive `depth` over the bound payloads returns 3
//     for `Pair(Leaf, Pair(Leaf, Leaf))`. The one indirection this compiler
//     introduces is an `enum` payload slot whose type reaches its own enum, and
//     a cycle with no such slot is refused BY NAME rather than handed to gcc:
//     `enum J { Num(i64), Arr([J; 4]) }` gives "recursive type `J` has no layout:
//     it stores itself by value (J -> J), and nothing on that cycle can stop ...
//     Break the cycle by routing it through an `enum` variant that can stop".
//   * `Vec<T>` is N14-09, owned by M8. `enum J { Num(i64), Arr(Vec<J>) }` is
//     accepted as a declaration; `Vec::new()` is "Undefined enum type: Vec".
//     THIS, not the recursion, is what keeps the arena alive.
//   * `(String, Json)` is N4-12, owned by M2 — tuples do not parse.
//
// So the tree lives in a fixed-capacity arena of parallel arrays inside one
// struct, addressed by i64 index, with -1 for "none". Exceeding the capacity is
// a parse error rather than a wrong answer.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct Json {
    src: String,
    len: i64,
    pos: i64,
    depth: i64,

    ok: bool,
    err: String,
    err_pos: i64,

    kind: [i64; 192],
    // Numeric value. Meaningful only where `exact` is 1 — see `number`.
    num: [i64; 192],
    exact: [i64; 192],
    // For strings: the decoded value. For numbers: the source lexeme.
    text: [String; 192],
    // For a member of an object: its key. Empty for array elements and the root.
    key: [String; 192],
    first: [i64; 192],
    last: [i64; 192],
    next: [i64; 192],
    count: i64,
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Cursor.
//
// WORKAROUND DISCHARGED (was UNOWNED (codegen) + N9-01): every function below
// used to take `mut j: Json` even when it only READ the document, because the
// shared-borrow spelling could not be FORWARDED — `fn a(p: &P) -> i64 { return
// b(p); }` emitted `b((*p))`, a dereference into a pointer parameter, and died
// in gcc. su2 (`b54502b`) found the cause: the call site decided whether to take
// an address from `param.mutable` alone, the declaration from `mutable` or a
// reference type. One predicate now, and the forward is a pointer: in this
// file's own emitted C, `peek` is `return __pd_Json_at(self, self->pos);` and
// `(*self)` occurs nowhere. So every reader below takes `&self` and only a
// writer takes `&mut self`. The `&` spelling is still declared by no row —
// N9-01 names `ref T`, which is M7's — so the gap closed and its ownership
// question did not.
//
// WORKAROUND DISCHARGED (was UNAPPLIED): every call was `js_thing(j, ...)`
// rather than `j.thing(...)`, after su2 had already made `&self` and `&mut self`
// parse, borrow-check and link. The rewrite is done: the cursor and the parser
// are methods on `Json`, in seven `impl Json` blocks beside their sections,
// called as `self.peek()` inside and `j.parse(...)` from outside. The rewrite was not
// free, and what it cost is the reason it is recorded here: it found two
// borrow-checker defects that no fixture had. Reading `self.pos` twice in one
// method was "Use of moved value: self.pos", because the checker did not see
// `Self` as `Json` and so could not tell an `i64` field is Copy — fixed by W1c
// (`d159139`, `ad63231`; tests/04_self_field_reads.pd). And a projection's
// move leaked from one function body into the next — fixed by W1d (merged at
// `cece734`; tests/regression/projection_move_scope.pd). The workaround was
// hiding both. tests/witness/json_parser.expected is byte-identical across
// the rewrite.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Free functions, not methods: these two classify a `char`, never the
// document, so there is no receiver for them to take. The same holds for every
// other free function below — `js_hex_digit`, `js_utf8`, `js_simple_escape`,
// `js_escape_into`, the kind codes and the two character codes — and for no
// function that reads or writes a `Json`.
fn js_is_ws(c: char) -> bool {
    // UNOWNED (char pattern) DISCHARGED: a char literal is a pattern now, so this
    // is the or-pattern the workaround described — `' ' | '\t' | '\n' | '\r'` —
    // rather than four `==` tests joined by `||`. The gap was never N6-07:
    // or-patterns already worked over an `i64`, and what was missing was the char
    // in pattern position, which is owned by nothing in
    // docs/contributing/1.0-requirements.tsv and is parked as issue #46.
    return match c {
        ' ' | '\t' | '\n' | '\r' => { true }
        _ => { false }
    };
}

fn js_is_digit(c: char) -> bool {
    // UNOWNED (char pattern) DISCHARGED: the range arm the workaround described,
    // `'0'..='9'`, ordered by code point. N6-03 was never the blocker — `48..=57`
    // over an `i64` already compiled — and the same char-in-pattern gap closed
    // both this and `js_is_ws` above.
    return match c {
        '0'..='9' => { true }
        _ => { false }
    };
}

impl Json {
    // N4-04: the cursor speaks `char`. Its end-of-input answer used to be -1, an
    // integer outside the char domain that only worked while a char WAS an integer.
    // NUL is the better answer and always was: `self.src` is a NUL-terminated C
    // string, so `'\0'` cannot occur inside it, and every comparison below fails
    // against it exactly as -1 did.
    fn at(&self, i: i64) -> char {
        if i < 0 { return '\0'; }
        if i >= self.len { return '\0'; }
        return string_char_at(self.src, i);
    }

    fn peek(&self) -> char {
        return self.at(self.pos);
    }

    fn bump(&mut self) {
        // N5-13 DISCHARGED: `+=` exists, so the cursor advance is one statement
        // instead of a name written twice.
        self.pos += 1;
    }

    fn fail(&mut self, msg: String) -> i64 {
        if self.ok {
            self.ok = false;
            self.err = msg;
            self.err_pos = self.pos;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    fn skip_ws(&mut self) {
        // N5-07 DISCHARGED: `loop` is a keyword, so the four unconditional loops in
        // this file say so instead of testing a constant. They read `while true {`;
        // `loop {` was `Expected ';' after expression, but found '{'`, because
        // `loop` lexed as an identifier. `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]*loop \{'` is 4 and
        // `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]*while true \{'` is 0.
        loop {
            if js_is_ws(self.peek()) {
                self.bump();
            } else {
                return;
            }
        }
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Arena.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

impl Json {
    fn alloc(&mut self, kind: i64) -> i64 {
        if self.count >= CAP_NODES {
            return self.fail("document has more than 192 nodes");
        }
        let i: i64 = self.count;
        self.count += 1;
        self.kind[i] = kind;
        self.num[i] = 0;
        self.exact[i] = 0;
        self.text[i] = "";
        self.key[i] = "";
        self.first[i] = -1;
        self.last[i] = -1;
        self.next[i] = -1;
        return i;
    }

    fn append(&mut self, parent: i64, child: i64) {
        if self.last[parent] < 0 {
            self.first[parent] = child;
        } else {
            self.next[self.last[parent]] = child;
        }
        self.last[parent] = child;
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Strings.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn js_hex_digit(c: char) -> i64 {
    if js_is_digit(c) {
        return c as i64 - '0' as i64;
    } else if c >= 'a' && c <= 'f' {
        return c as i64 - 'a' as i64 + 10;
    } else if c >= 'A' && c <= 'F' {
        return c as i64 - 'A' as i64 + 10;
    } else {
        return -1;
    }
}

impl Json {
    fn hex4(&mut self) -> i64 {
        let mut v: i64 = 0;
        let mut k: i64 = 0;
        while k < 4 {
            let d: i64 = js_hex_digit(self.peek());
            if d < 0 {
                return self.fail("\\u needs four hexadecimal digits");
            }
            // N5-12 DISCHARGED: `<<` and `|` are operators now, so the hex
            // accumulator is written as the shift and or it always was. It read
            // `v = v * 16 + d;` with a note that multiplication and addition
            // compute the same value here only because the operands are known to
            // be in range -- true, and an argument the reader had to check.
            v = (v << 4) | d;
            self.bump();
            k += 1;
        }
        return v;
    }
}

fn js_utf8(cp: i64) -> String {
    // N5-12 DISCHARGED here too: UTF-8 is defined in shifts and masks, and it
    // is written in them now. It read `cp / 64` for `cp >> 6` and `cp % 64` for
    // `cp & 63` -- equal for the non-negative `cp` this function is given, and
    // one more invariant the reader had to carry.
    if cp < 128 {
        return string_from_char(cp as char);
    }
    if cp < 2048 {
        let b1: String = string_from_char((192 | (cp >> 6)) as char);
        let b2: String = string_from_char((128 | (cp & 63)) as char);
        return string_concat(b1, b2);
    }
    if cp < 65536 {
        let c1: String = string_from_char((224 | (cp >> 12)) as char);
        let c2: String = string_from_char((128 | ((cp >> 6) & 63)) as char);
        let c3: String = string_from_char((128 | (cp & 63)) as char);
        return string_concat(string_concat(c1, c2), c3);
    }
    let d1: String = string_from_char((240 | (cp >> 18)) as char);
    let d2: String = string_from_char((128 | ((cp >> 12) & 63)) as char);
    let d3: String = string_from_char((128 | ((cp >> 6) & 63)) as char);
    let d4: String = string_from_char((128 | (cp & 63)) as char);
    return string_concat(string_concat(string_concat(d1, d2), d3), d4);
}

// Takes a `char` and answers a CODE POINT, or -1 for "not a simple escape".
// The return stays `i64` because -1 has to be outside the char domain to mean
// what it means, and the two callers test it with `>= 0`.
fn js_simple_escape(e: char) -> i64 {
    if e == '"' { return '"' as i64; }
    if e == '\\' { return '\\' as i64; }
    if e == '/' { return '/' as i64; }
    if e == 'b' { return ch_backspace() as i64; }
    if e == 'f' { return ch_formfeed() as i64; }
    if e == 'n' { return '\n' as i64; }
    if e == 'r' { return '\r' as i64; }
    if e == 't' { return '\t' as i64; }
    return -1;
}

impl Json {
    fn unicode_escape(&mut self) -> String {
        // Precondition: the cursor sits on the `u`.
        self.bump();
        let hi: i64 = self.hex4();
        if !self.ok { return ""; }
        if hi >= 55296 && hi <= 56319 {
            // High surrogate: RFC 8259 requires the low half to follow.
            if self.peek() != '\\' {
                self.fail("high surrogate is not followed by a low surrogate");
                return "";
            }
            self.bump();
            if self.peek() != 'u' {
                self.fail("high surrogate is not followed by a low surrogate");
                return "";
            }
            self.bump();
            let lo: i64 = self.hex4();
            if !self.ok { return ""; }
            if lo < 56320 || lo > 57343 {
                self.fail("high surrogate is not followed by a low surrogate");
                return "";
            }
            // (hi - 0xD800) << 10 | (lo - 0xDC00), plus 0x10000 — see N5-12 above.
            let cp: i64 = 65536 + (hi - 55296) * 1024 + (lo - 56320);
            return js_utf8(cp);
        }
        if hi >= 56320 && hi <= 57343 {
            self.fail("low surrogate with no high surrogate");
            return "";
        }
        if hi == 0 {
            // WORKAROUND UNOWNED: `String` is a NUL-terminated C `char*`
            // (`__pd_string_from_char` writes `result[0] = (char)c; result[1] = '\0';`),
            // so a document containing \u0000 cannot be represented, only refused.
            // No requirement row says what `String` is at the byte level; N4-05 is
            // one word, `String`, and is marked satisfied.
            self.fail("\\u0000 is not representable in a NUL-terminated String");
            return "";
        }
        return js_utf8(hi);
    }

    fn string_body(&mut self) -> String {
        // Precondition: the cursor sits on the opening quote.
        self.bump();
        let mut out: String = "";
        loop {
            let c: char = self.peek();
            if c == '\0' {
                self.fail("unterminated string");
                return "";
            }
            if c == '"' {
                self.bump();
                return out;
            }
            if c < ' ' {
                self.fail("unescaped control character in string");
                return "";
            }
            if c == '\\' {
                self.bump();
                let e: char = self.peek();
                if e == '\0' {
                    self.fail("unterminated escape");
                    return "";
                }
                let simple: i64 = js_simple_escape(e);
                if simple >= 0 {
                    self.bump();
                    out = string_concat(out, string_from_char(simple as char));
                } else if e == 'u' {
                    let decoded: String = self.unicode_escape();
                    if !self.ok { return ""; }
                    out = string_concat(out, decoded);
                } else {
                    self.fail("unknown escape");
                    return "";
                }
            } else {
                self.bump();
                out = string_concat(out, string_from_char(c));
            }
        }
        return "";
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Values.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

impl Json {
    fn literal(&mut self, word: String, kind: i64) -> i64 {
        let n: i64 = string_len(word);
        let mut i: i64 = 0;
        while i < n {
            if self.at(self.pos + i) != string_char_at(word, i) {
                return self.fail(string_concat("expected ", word));
            }
            i += 1;
        }
        self.pos = self.pos + n;
        return self.alloc(kind);
    }

    fn number(&mut self) -> i64 {
        let start: i64 = self.pos;
        let mut integral: bool = true;

        if self.peek() == '-' { self.bump(); }

        if self.peek() == '0' {
            self.bump();
        } else if js_is_digit(self.peek()) {
            while js_is_digit(self.peek()) { self.bump(); }
        } else {
            return self.fail("expected a digit");
        }

        if self.peek() == '.' {
            integral = false;
            self.bump();
            if !js_is_digit(self.peek()) {
                return self.fail("expected a digit after '.'");
            }
            while js_is_digit(self.peek()) { self.bump(); }
        }

        let e: char = self.peek();
        if e == 'e' || e == 'E' {
            integral = false;
            self.bump();
            let s: char = self.peek();
            if s == '+' || s == '-' { self.bump(); }
            if !js_is_digit(self.peek()) {
                return self.fail("expected a digit in the exponent");
            }
            while js_is_digit(self.peek()) { self.bump(); }
        }

        let node: i64 = self.alloc(k_number());
        if node < 0 { return -1; }
        let lexeme: String = string_substring(self.src, start, self.pos);
        self.text[node] = lexeme;
        // WORKAROUND UNOWNED: `f64` EXISTS. N4-02 is `satisfied` in
        // docs/contributing/1.0-requirements.tsv, and `let x: f64 = 1.5;` with
        // arithmetic and comparisons on it compiles. What is missing is any way to
        // move a float ACROSS the text boundary: `src/builtins.rs` declares no
        // `string_to_float` and no `float_to_string` — grep for `float` in that
        // file matches nothing at all — and N14's builtin set is closed, so NO
        // requirement row owns them. So a non-integral number has no value in this
        // program, only its source lexeme, which is what `render` prints. `exact`
        // records which is which, so a query for a numeric value can refuse rather
        // than return the truncated integer part.
        if integral {
            self.exact[node] = 1;
            self.num[node] = string_to_int(lexeme);
        }
        return node;
    }

    fn array(&mut self) -> i64 {
        self.bump();
        let node: i64 = self.alloc(k_array());
        if node < 0 { return -1; }
        self.skip_ws();
        if self.peek() == ']' {
            self.bump();
            return node;
        }
        loop {
            let child: i64 = self.value();
            if child < 0 { return -1; }
            self.append(node, child);
            self.skip_ws();
            let c: char = self.peek();
            if c == ',' {
                self.bump();
                self.skip_ws();
            } else if c == ']' {
                self.bump();
                return node;
            } else {
                return self.fail("expected ',' or ']'");
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    fn object(&mut self) -> i64 {
        self.bump();
        let node: i64 = self.alloc(k_object());
        if node < 0 { return -1; }
        self.skip_ws();
        if self.peek() == '}' {
            self.bump();
            return node;
        }
        loop {
            self.skip_ws();
            if self.peek() != '"' {
                return self.fail("expected a member name");
            }
            let name: String = self.string_body();
            if !self.ok { return -1; }
            self.skip_ws();
            if self.peek() != ':' {
                return self.fail("expected ':'");
            }
            self.bump();
            let child: i64 = self.value();
            if child < 0 { return -1; }
            self.key[child] = name;
            self.append(node, child);
            self.skip_ws();
            let c: char = self.peek();
            if c == ',' {
                self.bump();
            } else if c == '}' {
                self.bump();
                return node;
            } else {
                return self.fail("expected ',' or '}'");
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    fn value(&mut self) -> i64 {
        if !self.ok { return -1; }
        self.depth += 1;
        if self.depth > MAX_DEPTH {
            self.depth -= 1;
            return self.fail("nesting deeper than 24");
        }
        self.skip_ws();
        let c: char = self.peek();

        // N5-06 DISCHARGED: the chained form exists, so the staircases in this file
        // are one `if`, chained arms and one `else` at a single indent, rather than
        // nested `else` blocks descending a level per branch. Checkable two ways:
        // `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]*\} else if ' tests/witness/json_parser.pd` is 12,
        // and no `else` block in this file opens with an `if` as its first
        // statement, which is the shape the workaround had. It was 19 until the
        // dispatch below became a `match`, which is where seven of those arms were.
        //
        // UNOWNED (char pattern) DISCHARGED: this was the deepest staircase in the
        // file — nine branches, a mutable local and one assignment per arm — because
        // the byte it dispatches on is a `char` and `'n' => …` was "Expected pattern,
        // but found char 'n'". It is now the `match` the workaround described, as an
        // EXPRESSION whose value IS the node, so the local is no longer mutable and
        // no arm assigns. Neither N5-04 nor N6-02 was ever the blocker; both are
        // `satisfied` and always measured so. The `'-' | '0'..='9'` arm is the one
        // place the two ordered literal kinds meet a literal in one pattern.
        let node: i64 = match c {
            '\0' => { self.fail("unexpected end of input") }
            'n' => { self.literal("null", k_null()) }
            't' => { self.literal("true", k_true()) }
            'f' => { self.literal("false", k_false()) }
            '"' => {
                // The one arm that is not a single call: the decode has to happen
                // before the node exists, and a failed decode leaves -1. The local
                // is scoped to this arm rather than to the whole dispatch.
                let decoded: String = self.string_body();
                let mut n: i64 = -1;
                if self.ok {
                    n = self.alloc(k_string());
                    if n >= 0 { self.text[n] = decoded; }
                }
                n
            }
            '[' => { self.array() }
            '{' => { self.object() }
            '-' | '0'..='9' => { self.number() }
            // Exhaustiveness requires it, and it is where the `else` went: `char` is
            // not enumerable, so a dispatch over one is never complete without it.
            _ => { self.fail("unexpected character") }
        };

        self.depth -= 1;
        return node;
    }

    fn parse(&mut self, src: String) -> i64 {
        self.src = src;
        self.len = string_len(src);
        self.pos = 0;
        self.depth = 0;
        self.ok = true;
        self.err = "";
        self.err_pos = 0;
        self.count = 0;

        let root: i64 = self.value();
        if root < 0 { return -1; }
        self.skip_ws();
        if self.pos != self.len {
            return self.fail("trailing content after the document");
        }
        return root;
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Rendering — canonical re-serialisation, so the transcript shows the TREE and
// not the input echoed back.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn js_escape_into(s: String) -> String {
    let n: i64 = string_len(s);
    let mut out: String = "";
    let mut i: i64 = 0;
    while i < n {
        let c: char = string_char_at(s, i);
        if c == '"' {
            out = string_concat(out, "\\\"");
        } else if c == '\\' {
            out = string_concat(out, "\\\\");
        } else if c == '\n' {
            out = string_concat(out, "\\n");
        } else if c == '\r' {
            out = string_concat(out, "\\r");
        } else if c == '\t' {
            out = string_concat(out, "\\t");
        } else if c == ch_backspace() {
            out = string_concat(out, "\\b");
        } else if c == ch_formfeed() {
            out = string_concat(out, "\\f");
        } else {
            out = string_concat(out, string_from_char(c));
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

impl Json {
    fn render(&self, node: i64) -> String {
        let k: i64 = self.kind[node];
        if k == k_null() { return "null"; }
        if k == k_false() { return "false"; }
        if k == k_true() { return "true"; }
        if k == k_number() { return self.text[node]; }
        if k == k_string() {
            let body: String = js_escape_into(self.text[node]);
            return string_concat(string_concat("\"", body), "\"");
        }
        if k == k_array() {
            let mut out: String = "[";
            let mut child: i64 = self.first[node];
            let mut first: bool = true;
            while child >= 0 {
                if !first { out = string_concat(out, ","); }
                first = false;
                out = string_concat(out, self.render(child));
                child = self.next[child];
            }
            return string_concat(out, "]");
        }
        let mut out2: String = "{";
        let mut member: i64 = self.first[node];
        let mut first2: bool = true;
        while member >= 0 {
            if !first2 { out2 = string_concat(out2, ","); }
            first2 = false;
            let name: String = js_escape_into(self.key[member]);
            out2 = string_concat(out2, string_concat(string_concat("\"", name), "\":"));
            out2 = string_concat(out2, self.render(member));
            member = self.next[member];
        }
        return string_concat(out2, "}");
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Queries.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

impl Json {
    fn member(&self, node: i64, name: String) -> i64 {
        if self.kind[node] != k_object() { return -1; }
        let mut child: i64 = self.first[node];
        while child >= 0 {
            if string_eq(self.key[child], name) { return child; }
            child = self.next[child];
        }
        return -1;
    }

    fn element(&self, node: i64, index: i64) -> i64 {
        if self.kind[node] != k_array() { return -1; }
        let mut child: i64 = self.first[node];
        let mut i: i64 = 0;
        while child >= 0 {
            if i == index { return child; }
            i += 1;
            child = self.next[child];
        }
        return -1;
    }

    fn length(&self, node: i64) -> i64 {
        let mut child: i64 = self.first[node];
        let mut n: i64 = 0;
        while child >= 0 {
            n += 1;
            child = self.next[child];
        }
        return n;
    }

    fn kind_name(&self, node: i64) -> String {
        if node < 0 { return "absent"; }
        let k: i64 = self.kind[node];
        if k == k_null() { return "null"; }
        if k == k_false() { return "bool"; }
        if k == k_true() { return "bool"; }
        if k == k_number() { return "number"; }
        if k == k_string() { return "string"; }
        if k == k_array() { return "array"; }
        return "object";
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Driver.
//
// `show_ok` and `show_err` stay free functions taking `mut j: Json`, and that
// is not the cursor workaround surviving. They are the transcript harness, not
// part of the document's interface, and they WRITE the document — `parse`
// resets it — so `mut` here is the in-out parameter it means, which lowers to
// `struct Json*` and is exactly what a writer should take.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn show_ok(mut j: Json, label: String, src: String) {
    let root: i64 = j.parse(src);
    if root < 0 {
        print(string_concat(string_concat(label, " UNEXPECTED ERROR "), j.err));
        return;
    }
    print(string_concat(string_concat(label, " "), j.render(root)));
}

fn show_err(mut j: Json, label: String, src: String) {
    let root: i64 = j.parse(src);
    if root >= 0 {
        print(string_concat(label, " UNEXPECTEDLY ACCEPTED"));
        return;
    }
    let at: String = int_to_string(j.err_pos);
    print(string_concat(string_concat(string_concat(label, " at "), at), string_concat(": ", j.err)));
}

fn main() {
    let mut j: Json = Json {
        src: "",
        len: 0,
        pos: 0,
        depth: 0,
        ok: true,
        err: "",
        err_pos: 0,
        kind: [0; 192],
        num: [0; 192],
        exact: [0; 192],
        text: [""; 192],
        key: [""; 192],
        first: [0; 192],
        last: [0; 192],
        next: [0; 192],
        count: 0,
    };

    print("-- accepted --");
    show_ok(j, "scalars   ", "[null, true, false, 0, -17, 3.25, 1e3, 2E-2]");
    show_ok(j, "strings   ", "[\"\", \"a\\\"b\", \"sl\\/ash\", \"\\u0041\\u00e9\\u20ac\"]");
    show_ok(j, "surrogate ", "[\"\\ud83d\\ude00\"]");

    show_ok(j, "tab escape", "[\"tab\\there\"]");
    show_ok(j, "nesting   ", "{\"a\":{\"b\":[1,[2,[3,{}]]]},\"c\":[]}");
    show_ok(j, "whitespace", "  {  \"k\"  :  [ 1 , 2 ]  }  ");
    show_ok(j, "dup keys  ", "{\"k\":1,\"k\":2}");

    print("-- queries --");
    let doc: i64 = j.parse("{\"name\":\"pd\",\"tags\":[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"],\"n\":42,\"ratio\":0.5}");
    print(string_concat("root kind      ", j.kind_name(doc)));
    print(string_concat("member count   ", int_to_string(j.length(doc))));
    print(string_concat("name           ", j.text[j.member(doc, "name")]));
    let tags: i64 = j.member(doc, "tags");
    print(string_concat("tags length    ", int_to_string(j.length(tags))));
    print(string_concat("tags[1]        ", j.text[j.element(tags, 1)]));
    let n: i64 = j.member(doc, "n");
    print(string_concat("n              ", int_to_string(j.num[n])));
    let ratio: i64 = j.member(doc, "ratio");
    print(string_concat("ratio lexeme   ", j.text[ratio]));
    // The UNOWNED float↔text boundary gap again, as an observable: only text, no
    // value. Nothing turns the lexeme "0.5" into an f64, and nothing would print
    // one back out, so `exact` is 0 and the lexeme is all this program can carry.
    print(string_concat("ratio exact    ", int_to_string(j.exact[ratio])));
    // UNOWNED (codegen) + N9-01 DISCHARGED here too: `member` and `kind_name`
    // only read, so both take `&self`, and nesting them is two SHARED borrows of
    // `j` in one expression, which the borrow checker accepts. It was a temp,
    // `let missing: i64 = js_member(j, doc, "nope");`, because both took `mut`
    // and two mutable borrows in one expression were rightly "Conflicting
    // borrows: cannot borrow `j` because it is already mutably borrowed".
    print(string_concat("missing        ", j.kind_name(j.member(doc, "nope"))));
    print(string_concat("nodes used     ", int_to_string(j.count)));

    print("-- refused --");
    show_err(j, "empty        ", "");
    show_err(j, "trailing     ", "1 2");
    show_err(j, "unterminated ", "\"abc");
    show_err(j, "control char ", "\"a\tb\"");
    show_err(j, "bad escape   ", "\"a\\qb\"");
    show_err(j, "short \\u     ", "\"\\u12\"");
    show_err(j, "lone surrog. ", "\"\\ud83d\"");
    show_err(j, "nul escape   ", "\"\\u0000\"");
    show_err(j, "leading zero ", "01");
    show_err(j, "bare dot     ", "1.");
    show_err(j, "no exponent  ", "1e");
    show_err(j, "missing comma", "[1 2]");
    show_err(j, "missing colon", "{\"a\" 1}");
    show_err(j, "bad member   ", "{a:1}");
    show_err(j, "junk         ", "@");
}
